    "lawCode": "Amd §227-a, RP L",
    "lawSection": "Real Property Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S3891\n\nSPONSOR: SEPULVEDA\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the real property law, in relation to\nindividuals with disabilities being able to terminate their leases when\nmoving to a residence of a family member or entering certain facilities\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo allow those with a disability to more easily move into housing that\nbetter suits their needs.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends the real property law by amending section 227-a.  Para-\ngraph (a) of subdivision 1 of section 227-a is amended to include people\nwith disabilities in addition to those who are 62 years old or older\nduring a lease agreement for the purpose of terminating a lease when the\nindividual can no longer live independently.\n\nQualifying conditions include: change in financial circumstances that is\nrelated to a disability; certification by a physician that a person is\nno longer able to live independently; certification by a physician that\na person's housing is currently unsuitable due to the structure of the\nhousing and medical reasons; or when a person is notified that they may\nlive in an adult care facility, a residential healthcare facility,\nsupportive housing, or subsidized housing.\n\nAny lease that is terminated under paragraph (b) subdivision one of this\nsection may be terminated in writing and shall be effective no earlier\nthan 30 days after the date on which the next rental payment subsequent\nto the date when the notice is delivered is due. The notice must be\naccompanied by a physician's certification.  Prospective tenants who may\nqualify under this act shall receive written notice of their rights upon\napplication.\n\nSection 2 provides that the act shall take effect immediately and will\napply to leases or rental agreements entered after the effective date.\n\n \nEXISTING LAW:\n\nCurrently, people over the age of 62 may terminate a lease early if they\nare moving into certain facilities or with family (see § 227-a and §\n227-b of the Real Property Law) and victims of domestic violence may do\nso under certain circumstances as well (see § 227-c and § 227-d of the\nReal Property Law).\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nAs the law stands today, when a person has a sudden accident that leaves\nthem disabled and unable to use their current housing, they must contin-\nue to pay rent while at the same time seeking an alternative place to\nlive. This legislation would give tenants a way out from under a lease\nthat they can no longer carry due to circumstances that are outside\ntheir control.\n\nHowever, while the example above offers a stark example for why this\nlegislation is necessary, not all changes in disability status are so\nextreme. This bill encompasses any change in disability status that\nwould render a person's current housing unsuitable, as certified by a\nphysician. In addition, it provides for situations where a person's\ndisability status may not have changed at all, but a better option such\nas an assisted living facility or subsidized housing becomes available\nto them.\n\nIn the same way that we provide protections to the elderly and to\nvictims of domestic violence, we should also protect people with disa-\nbilities from getting stuck in unsuitable housing due to a continuing\nlease.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2017-18: S4970-A - referred to Judiciary/ A.6029-C - passed assembly\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
